Let's look at the interval test file, and the changelog entry, and datetime.js/duration.js fromISO handling.

The bug: In `Interval.fromISO`, when `Settings.throwOnInvalid` is true, `DateTime.fromISO(s, opts)` will throw InvalidDateTimeError before Interval.invalid is called. Wait, the test is `Interval.fromISO works with Settings.throwOnInvalid`. Let's think about what the expected behavior is. Let's check the actual upstream fix. In Luxon, the fix (commit) was in interval.js fromISO:

Upstream luxon code (v1.21+):

```js
  static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      let start, startIsValid;
      try {
        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
        startIsValid = start.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        startIsValid = false;
      }

      let end, endIsValid;
      try {
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
        endIsValid = end.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        endIsValid = false;
      }

      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (startIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (endIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed asISO 8601`);
  }
```

Yes! That's the upstream fix (changelog: "Fixed throwOnInvalid logic for Interval"). The issue: when throwOnInvalid is true, DateTime.fromISO throws instead of returning invalid, so the try/catch is needed so the fallback path (duration parsing) still works and eventually Interval.invalid throws InvalidIntervalError.

